	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44274</id><title>Prioritize Multilateralism to Resolve Iran War Deepening Humanitarian Crisis, Says OSF Vice President</title><updated>2026-10-06T17:42:43-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44274" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/OSF.png" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/OSF.png" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/OSF.png" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/OSF.png&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, October 6 (IPS)  - Seven months into the Iran War, threats of further military escalation and stalled efforts to reopen the Strait of Hormuz have heightened fears of worsening humanitarian suffering and wider regional instability.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44274&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Prioritize Multilateralism to Resolve Iran War Deepening Humanitarian Crisis, Says OSF Vice President”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44269</id><title>Dementia: A Rapidly Rising Global Challenge</title><updated>2026-10-06T11:13:01-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44269" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/dementiafeatured.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/dementiafeatured.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/dementiafeatured-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/dementiafeatured.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
PORTLAND, USA, October 6 (IPS)  - Dementia is a rapidly growing global health challenge that affects many millions of people worldwide and places significant economic, healthcare, and social strains on societies, particularly as populations age.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44269&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Dementia: A Rapidly Rising Global Challenge”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44268</id><title>Workers Helping Feed the World but Starved of Dignity</title><updated>2026-10-06T06:53:35-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44268" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/An-IPES-Food-report-warns-that-workers-in-the-global-food-system-face-high-exploitation-in-keeping-the-world-fed-credit-Busani-Bafana-IPS.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/An-IPES-Food-report-warns-that-workers-in-the-global-food-system-face-high-exploitation-in-keeping-the-world-fed-credit-Busani-Bafana-IPS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/An-IPES-Food-report-warns-that-workers-in-the-global-food-system-face-high-exploitation-in-keeping-the-world-fed-credit-Busani-Bafana-IPS-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/An-IPES-Food-report-warns-that-workers-in-the-global-food-system-face-high-exploitation-in-keeping-the-world-fed-credit-Busani-Bafana-IPS.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
BULAWAYO, October 6 (IPS)  - A new report reveals widespread labour exploitation among food system workers, warning that keeping the world fed comes at the cost of human dignity.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44268&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Workers Helping Feed the World but Starved of Dignity”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44267</id><title>The Late Arrivals</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:20:44-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44267" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2025/11/James-Alix-Michel_200-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;
VICTORIA, Seychelles, October 6 (IPS)  - One night, many years ago now, on a remote beach in the outer islands of Seychelles, I sat in the darkness and watched a green turtle come ashore. She had waited for the heat of the day to leave the sand. Under a sky crowded with stars, she hauled herself slowly above the tideline, dug with a patience no human hand could match, and laid her eggs. Then, in the small hours, she turned back toward the water. She did not hurry. She paused once, as though listening, and then made her slow, unhesitating way down the sand, back to a home older than any I could imagine.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44267&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The Late Arrivals”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44276</id><title>From drought to deluge: ‘Super’ El Niño’s expanding reach</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44276" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/15-09-2026_UNICEF_Nepal-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Across Papua New Guinea, water sources are drying up, crops are failing and food prices are rising months before the worst agricultural impacts are expected. Behind those warning signs is an El Niño whose Pacific Ocean temperatures have already surpassed the equivalent measurements during the historic 1997 and 2015 events – part of an unfolding climate pattern being dubbed a “Super El Niño” by some climate watchers.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44276&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “From drought to deluge: ‘Super’ El Niño’s expanding reach”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44275</id><title>Djibouti faces ‘overlapping emergencies’ as Yemen’s refugee exodus continues</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44275" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;Djibouti is facing “overlapping emergencies” as refugees fleeing the conflict in Yemen arrive on its shores while migrants continue to travel in the opposite direction, the UN migration agency (IOM) warned on Tuesday. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44275&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Djibouti faces ‘overlapping emergencies’ as Yemen’s refugee exodus continues”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44273</id><title>Testing and monitoring underway in suspected Russia plague death</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44273" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/20-04-2022-Russia-Lake-Baikal-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/20-04-2022-Russia-Lake-Baikal-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/20-04-2022-Russia-Lake-Baikal-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/20-04-2022-Russia-Lake-Baikal-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The death of a lab worker in Russia’s Siberia region has sparked concerns over a possible plague case, the UN World Health Organization (WHO) said on Tuesday, adding that it was following the situation closely.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44273&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Testing and monitoring underway in suspected Russia plague death”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44272</id><title>In Islamabad, Guterres calls for end to US-Iran conflict</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44272" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/06-10-2026_SG_Pakistan_Presser.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/06-10-2026_SG_Pakistan_Presser.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/06-10-2026_SG_Pakistan_Presser.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/06-10-2026_SG_Pakistan_Presser.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN Secretary-General appealed on Tuesday for an end to the United States-Iran war and the toll it has taken on global trade, as he began an official visit to Pakistan.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44272&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “In Islamabad, Guterres calls for end to US-Iran conflict”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44271</id><title>‘Ebola takes the people children depend on’: UNICEF</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44271" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/21-09-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/21-09-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/21-09-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/21-09-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Ebola outbreak in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) is robbing children of their loved ones – with some succumbing to the deadly disease themselves, the UN Children’s Fund (UNICEF) reported on Tuesday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44271&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Ebola takes the people children depend on’: UNICEF”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44270</id><title>When streets become pharmacies: Booming illicit markets in Central Africa</title><updated>2026-10-06T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44270" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2015/09/22531/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2015/09/22531/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2015/09/22531/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2015/09/22531/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Antibiotics, malaria treatments, painkillers, opioids. More than 250 tonnes of medical products were seized over six years in Central Africa. Behind those numbers is an illicit market that thrives not simply despite the region’s fragile health systems, but in part because of them.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/06/44270&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “When streets become pharmacies: Booming illicit markets in Central Africa”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0007s ch:1 -->